# Line endings, decided per file type -- and this one is a functional setting,
# not cosmetics.
#
# cmd.exe's `goto :label` scans the batch file for its labels, and on a batch
# file whose lines end with a bare LF the scan loses labels partway through the
# file: the run dies with "The system cannot find the batch label specified -
# waitboot" and, when root.bat was double-clicked (which runs it under
# `cmd /c`), the console closed on that line and looked like "it flashes and
# quits". GitHub's "Download ZIP" ships the stored bytes, so whoever downloaded
# the archive instead of cloning got LF and could not run root.bat at all.
#
# `-text` therefore means: store and hand out these files EXACTLY as committed.
# root.bat is committed with CRLF, so clone, `git archive` and ZIP all get CRLF.
*.bat  -text
*.cmd  -text

# Shell scripts and C sources must stay LF: a CR would end up inside the
# shebang, inside `printf` payloads and inside the .incbin paths.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.c    text eol=lf
*.h    text eol=lf
*.S    text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf

# Never let anyone touch a binary artifact.
*.so              -text
*.img             -text
*.png             -text
*.jpg             -text
*.webp            -text
su_daemon_arm32   -text
*.gz              -text
*.xz              -text
*.zip             -text
